problem_statement:
Theme name, fonts and colorScheme selectors can break out of the generated CSS

`stylesheet(theme, options)` writes the theme's name (into a CSS comment), `fonts.body` / `fonts.numeric` (into declarations) and the `colorScheme` selectors straight into the CSS it returns. Themes can come from data, so a hostile value can close the comment, end a declaration or rule, or even end the surrounding `<style>` element.

Reproduce:

```ts
stylesheet({ ...defaultTheme, name: "x */ body{display:none} /*" });
stylesheet({ ...defaultTheme, fonts: { body: "x </style><script>alert(1)</script>" } });
stylesheet(defaultTheme, { colorScheme: { dark: "x; } body { color: red" } });
defineTheme({ ...defaultTheme, name: "x */ body{display:none} /*" });
```

All of these succeed and the payload lands verbatim in the CSS.

Expected: `defineTheme` should refuse a theme name or font containing characters that could end a CSS comment, declaration, rule or the `<style>` element (`<`, `>`, `{`, `}`, `;`, `\`, `/*`, `*/`), and `stylesheet()` should validate the theme it is given (as `defineTheme` does) and also check each `colorScheme` selector. The thrown error message should say the text is "not allowed in CSS". Ordinary values must keep working, e.g. a name like `Lab (2026)`, normal font stacks with quotes and commas, and ordinary selectors such as `[data-theme="dark"]`.

